course: biology
lesson: 230
title: Echolocation in the sea and in people: dolphins, whales, swiftlets and clicking humans
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Bats are not the only animals that find their way with echoes; dolphins, many whales, some cave birds, shrews and even some blind people do it too.
explain: Under the sea light fades fast, but sound travels well and quickly, so an echo is often the best way to «see» there.
explain: A dolphin in murky water clicks, the clicks bounce off a fish, and the echoes come back through its lower jaw to its ears.
explain: Think of the sonar screen on a fishing boat on TV: the boat sends a ping and times the echo, a trick dolphins had for millions of years before us.
fact: Dolphins echolocate with clicks made in their nose passages and focused by the fatty «melon» in the forehead; echoes return through the lower jaw to the ears.
fact: A dolphin's echoes can tell whether an object is hollow or solid, so it can find a hidden ring in water too cloudy to see through.
fact: Sperm whales hunt squid more than a kilometre down in total darkness with clicks that are among the loudest sounds any animal makes.
fact: Sonar sends a ping and times the echo; sound travels about four times faster in water than in air.
fact: Some blind people, such as Daniel Kish, learn to echolocate with tongue clicks, and brain scans show the «seeing» part of their brain at work.
fact: Some cave swiftlets of Asia and the oilbird of South America click to find their way in pitch-black caves, with clicks low enough for people to hear.
fact: Some shrews and tenrecs use simple echoes from squeaks to explore tunnels and thick grass.
fact: A beluga whale can change the shape of its melon to aim its clicks, and it uses echoes to find breathing holes in Arctic ice.
fact: Loud noise from ships and underwater building can drown out whale and dolphin calls; after 11 September 2001, when few ships sailed for a few days, scientists found right whales were calmer and less stressed.
fact: Fish do not talk, but many make sounds by drumming muscles on their swim bladder, grinding teeth or rubbing bones, and a coral reef at night is noisy with the crackle of snapping shrimps and the grunts of fish.
fact: Toothed whales like dolphins, orcas and sperm whales echolocate; baleen whales like the blue whale and humpback do not seem to, because they gulp huge mouthfuls of tiny krill instead of chasing single fast prey.
fact: Some people say dolphins can tell if a woman is going to have a baby by echoes; dolphin clicks do pass a little way into a body, but this has not been properly proven.
exercise: How can dolphins find a ring hidden in murky water? | They click and listen to the echoes | They smell it from across the pool | They see straight through the mud with their sharp eyes | They feel it with their tails
exercise: Where are a dolphin's clicks focused? | Through the fatty melon in its forehead | Through the wide fluke at the end of its tail | Through its blowhole only | Through its eyes
exercise: How do echoes reach a dolphin's ears? | Through its lower jaw | Through its dorsal fin | Through its blowhole on top | Through its long tail
exercise: What can a dolphin's echoes tell about an object? | Whether it is hollow or solid | What colour it is painted on the outside | What it would taste like to eat | How many years old it is, exactly
exercise: How does a sperm whale find squid in the black deep? | With very loud clicks and their echoes | With big glowing eyes that light up the water | By following the light down | By smelling the squid
exercise: How does a ship's sonar work? | It sends a ping and times the echo | It films the sea floor with bright lights | It listens to the fish talking to each other | It sends out magnets that stick to metal
exercise: Why is sound better than light for finding things deep in the sea? | Light fades quickly, while sound travels far and fast in water | Sound is brighter than light under the water | Light cannot enter water at all, not even at the very top of the sea | Fish are scared of light
exercise: How does Daniel Kish find his way without sight? | By clicking his tongue and listening to echoes | By smelling the walls | By counting his steps only | By feeling the wind on his hands and face as he walks along
exercise: Which birds click to find their way in dark caves? | Some cave swiftlets and the oilbird | Owls and eagles that hunt late at night | Penguins and puffins | Parrots and crows
exercise: Can people hear the swiftlets' clicks? | Yes, their clicks are low enough for us to hear | No, they are ultrasound, far too high for human ears | Only dogs can hear them | Only under water
exercise: Why does a shrew squeak as it runs through grass? | Simple echoes help it sense what is close | It is singing to the worms | It is scaring off the owls that hunt it at night | It is calling its mother every time
exercise: What can a beluga do with its forehead? | Change the shape of its melon to aim its clicks | Break thick ice by headbutting it | Store food in it | Breathe through it
exercise: Why do belugas need echolocation under Arctic ice? | To find breathing holes in the ice | To keep warm in the freezing cold water | To find their way to warm southern seas | To light up the dark water under the ice so they can see
exercise: How does ship noise harm whales and dolphins? | It drowns out their calls | It makes them grow much faster | It helps them find more food | It does not affect them at all
exercise: How do many fish make sounds? | By drumming muscles on their swim bladder or grinding teeth | By talking with their lips | By blowing tiny whistles from their gills each time they breathe | They never make any sound
exercise: Which of these whales echolocate? | Toothed whales like dolphins, orcas and sperm whales | Only blue whales | Only humpback whales, when they sing their long songs | All whales and all fish
test: In a cartoon a fish chats in a squeaky voice. What do real fish do? | They cannot talk, but many grunt, click or drum | They talk quietly to each other in real words, like us | They stay silent all their lives | They sing like birds
test: «a blind guy on the news rides a BIKE?? how» What is the best answer? | He clicks his tongue and reads the echoes, like a bat | He can secretly see | The bike drives itself | He uses a map in his head only, and remembers every bump in the road
test: Try it: shut your eyes and walk slowly towards a wall while humming. What may you notice? | You can often hear when the wall is close | Nothing at all, ever | The wall hums back a tune | Your eyes see through your eyelids
test: What would happen if a dolphin's lower jaw could not pass sound? | It would hear its echoes badly and find food harder | It would swim backwards | It could no longer breathe | Nothing, because dolphins hear only through their tails
test: A blue whale and a sperm whale are side by side in a book. Which one hunts with echoes? | The sperm whale, a toothed whale | The blue whale, a baleen whale | Both of them | Neither of them
test: «why don't blue whales need clicks, they're massive» What is the best answer? | They gulp huge mouthfuls of tiny krill, not single fast prey | They are too lazy to click | They see perfectly in the dark sea, so they never need any echoes | They eat only plants
test: After 11 September 2001 there were fewer ships for a few days. What did scientists find in right whales? | Calmer stress levels | More stress than before | They forgot their songs | They swam into the empty ports
test: «that dolphin's staring at my mum, creepy» What might really be going on? | It may be clicking at her, and echoes pass a little way into her body | Dolphins stare to hypnotise people | It wants to bite her | Dolphins cannot see people
test: A friend says people invented echolocation with sonar. What is wrong with that? | Bats and dolphins used it for tens of millions of years before us | Nothing, only people use it | Sonar was copied from fish that talk to each other on the coral reefs | Sonar uses light, not sound
test: Count them: 3 dolphins and 2 orcas swim past. How many blowholes, and how many tails? | 5 blowholes and 5 tails | 10 blowholes and 5 tails | 5 blowholes and 10 tails | 3 blowholes and 5 tails
test: A coral reef is recorded at night. What will the recording sound like? | Noisy with crackles from shrimps and grunts from fish | Totally silent | Like birds singing | Only like waves breaking far away on the beach
exam: Identify the anatomical structure through which the returning echoes are conveyed to the ear of a dolphin. | The lower jaw | The dorsal fin | The tail fluke | The blowhole
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capacity for echolocation amongst whales? | It is possessed by toothed whales but apparently not by baleen whales | It is possessed by all whales without exception | It is possessed by baleen whales alone | It is possessed by no whale whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning human echolocation. | Certain blind persons perceive their surroundings by means of tongue clicks and their echoes | No human being is capable of perceiving echoes | Human echolocation requires an implanted device | It is possible only beneath the surface of water
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which sound is preferred to light for the detection of objects in the deep sea. | Light is rapidly extinguished in water, whereas sound is propagated far and swiftly | Sound is visible to marine animals | Light travels more slowly in water than sound | Water amplifies light but absorbs sound, thereby rendering sound of little use at great depth
